Q: Why is BigInt not a constructor function, unlike Number, Boolean and String? A: <p>The <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/BigInt#constructor" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>BigInt</code> function</a> actually still is or qualifies fully as built-in constructor (a claim backed by arguments, evidence and example code further down). It just is implemented in a way that it throws when being invoked with the <code>new</code> operator. The linked example code at MDN shows exemplarily how one, e.g., by consulting the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/new.target#try_it" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>new.target</code> meta-property</a> can deny the further execution within the body of an otherwise perfectly constructable good old ES3 function-type.</p>
<p>We need to take following into consideration.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>invoking <code>new BigInt(0)</code> and raising a <code>TypeError</code> does not mean ... <em>&quot;not a constructor&quot;</em>. Two different mechanisms make a callable-type reject <code>new</code>, and the OP's question conflates them.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Mechanism 1 - no <code>[[Construct]]</code> slot at all: arrows-functions, concise-methods, global built-in functions like <code>parseInt</code>, <code>Math.max</code> etc.; all are rejected up front upon the specs <code>IsConstructor</code> predicate.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Mechanism 2 - a present, but guarded <code>[[Construct]]</code> slot: <code>IsConstructor</code> answers <code>true</code> for <code>Symbol</code> and <code>BigInt</code> as well and <code>new</code> enters the function's body, but the first/next step throws on <code>new.target</code> NOT being <code>undefined</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>There is further prove, the <code>[[Construct]]</code> slot is real and not asserted: <code>class X extends BigInt {}</code> is a legal definition. An <code>extends</code> clause demands a constructor, so the slot must be there. One even can implement a <code>hasConstructSlot</code> predicate function (as will be demonstrated further down) which uses a <code>Proxy</code>'s construct-trap. A verifying probe will inherit <code>[[Construct]]</code> only from a target that has one.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Following conclusion(s) then are most likely to be valid: The wrapper-object constructors (like with e.g., <code>new Number(5) -&gt; </code>typeof &quot;object&quot;<code>or a truthy</code>new Boolean(false)<code>) are legacy footguns kept only for web-compatibility. **</code>Symbol<code>and</code>BigInt<code>arrived with no legacy to preserve**, so TC39 kept the same machinery ... an own non-writable</code>prototype<code>property and a boxing target for e.g.</code>Object(42n)`...  but did shut down the only nonsensical door (see #2 further down), a hand-built boxed primitive.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
